.TH TIDYMAC 1 "September 2026" "Tidy for Mac" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tidymac \- a friendly, safe cleanup and speed-up for your Mac
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tidymac scan
[\fB\-\-card\fR \fIname\fR ...] [\fB\-\-json\fR]
.br
.B tidymac up
(\fB\-\-safe\fR | \fB\-\-card\fR \fIname\fR ... | \fB\-\-all\-checked\fR) [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR]
.br
.B tidymac speed
[\fB\-\-card\fR \fIname\fR ...] [\fB\-\-all\fR] [\fB\-\-updates\fR] [\fB\-\-app\-updates\fR] [\fB\-\-bench\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR]
.br
.B tidymac receipts
[\fB\-\-json\fR]
.br
.B tidymac restore
\fIsession\-id\fR | \fBlast\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR]
.br
.B tidymac trash
[\fB\-\-empty\fR \fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR]
.br
.B tidymac disk
[\fB\-\-json\fR]
.br
.B tidymac money
[\fB\-\-speedtest\fR] [\fB\-\-plan\fR \fImbps\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR]
.br
.B tidymac sort
[\fB\-\-desktop\fR] [\fB\-\-downloads\fR] [\fB\-\-older\-than\fR \fIdays\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR]
.br
.B tidymac report
[\fB\-\-out\fR \fIfile\fR] [\fB\-\-md\fR] [\fB\-\-open\fR]
.br
.B tidymac screenshots
\fIdirectory\fR
.br
.B tidymac
\fBhelp\fR | \fBversion\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tidymac
is the command-line face of Tidy for Mac. It shares every scanner and action with the app, so the
two never disagree. Nothing is deleted outright: everything goes to the Trash, every action writes
a receipt, and
.B tidymac restore
puts a session back while its items are still in the Trash. No command ever asks for an
administrator password; where one is needed the command to run is printed instead.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B scan
Show what could be tidied, card by card. Changes nothing. Items marked \fB[x]\fR are pre-checked
(safe to move), \fB[ ]\fR are for you to decide, and a shield marks protected items that never move.
.TP
.B up
Move the pre-checked items of the chosen cards to the Trash and save a receipt.
\fB\-\-safe\fR selects caches, logs, installers and developer caches, the same set the app's weekly
quiet tidy uses. In a terminal it asks before moving; from a script it exits 3 unless \fB\-\-yes\fR
is given. \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR shows the plan and moves nothing. Items not pre-checked in the app are
never moved.
.TP
.B speed
Print the health verdict and the Speed cards. The exit status is the verdict: 0 green, 1 yellow,
2 red. Slow cards (\fBupdates\fR, \fBapp\-updates\fR, \fBbench\fR) run only when named or flagged.
\fB\-\-all\fR also runs the startup card, which asks System Events for login items.
.TP
.B receipts
List every past session, newest first, with its id.
.TP
.B restore
Put a session's items back where they were. Accepts an id prefix or \fBlast\fR.
.TP
.B trash
Show the Trash size. With \fB\-\-empty \-\-yes\fR, ask Finder to empty it.
.TP
.B disk
Print the boot-volume map from the physical disk down to the sealed snapshot macOS booted from.
.TP
.B money
The Money tab as text: the "is this Mac done" verdict, battery decision, subscription apps with
last-opened dates, cloud-drive overlap, what a tidy would free, built-in alternatives.
\fB\-\-speedtest\fR downloads from a nearby server for a few seconds; \fB\-\-plan\fR compares
with the speed on your bill.
.TP
.B sort
File loose Desktop and Downloads files older than \fIdays\fR (default 7) into typed folders under a
"Tidied" folder in the same place. Moves, never deletes; a receipt is saved so \fBrestore\fR undoes it.
.TP
.B report
Write the monthly checkup as one HTML page (\fB\-\-md\fR for Markdown; \fB\-\-out \-\fR for stdout).
Default file: ~/Desktop/Tidy checkup <date>.html.
.TP
.B screenshots
Render every window with sample data into PNG files in \fIdirectory\fR.
.SH CARDS
Scan and up:
.B caches logs installers dev claude duplicates leftovers unused backups big
.PP
Speed:
.B health now startup browsers wifi sync timemachine rosetta menubar printers power sleep safety batteries defaults
and, on request,
.B updates app\-updates bench
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine-readable output on every command. Sizes are bytes, dates ISO 8601.
.TP
.B \-\-yes
Skip the confirmation for \fBup\fR and \fBtrash \-\-empty\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Show what \fBup\fR would move and move nothing.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, or a green verdict from \fBspeed\fR.
.TP
.B 1
\fBspeed\fR: yellow verdict.
.TP
.B 2
\fBspeed\fR: red verdict.
.TP
.B 3
Confirmation needed; run again with \fB\-\-yes\fR.
.TP
.B 64
Usage error.
.TP
.B 70
The command ran but failed.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/Library/Application Support/Tidy Mac/history.json
Receipts of every session.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
tidymac scan
tidymac up \-\-safe \-\-dry\-run
tidymac up \-\-safe \-\-yes \-\-json
tidymac speed \-\-card safety \-\-json | jq '.cards.safety[] | select(.ok == false)'
tidymac restore last
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
The app: Tidy for Mac in your Applications folder. Full documentation in docs/CLI.md.
